In mobile-edge federated learning, uplink links are time-varying and deadline-bound, and full-parameter updates frequently miss their deadlines when links degrade. Federated LoRA reduces the payload, but existing systems treat the rank as a training hyperparameter, ignoring the coupling that the rank itself determines the payload and the payload determines the delivery probability: a larger rank buys adaptation capacity but is more likely to miss the deadline. We formalize rank selection and client admission as a joint scheduling problem under deadline constraints and propose FedRAS, which scores every client--rank pair with a payload-conditioned delivery risk and a saturating capacity gain, solves the assignment exactly with a dynamic program under a byte budget and a participation cap, and aggregates heterogeneous ranks with frozen-factor rank masking. The design comes with mechanism-level guarantees: the grid assignment is exact on the stated budget grid, the budget regime in which a per-client greedy rule is already optimal is characterized in closed form, and frozen-factor aggregation of heterogeneous ranks is exact. The gains come from delivering more updates within the deadline rather than from changing training, which is why accuracy remains statistically equivalent while the network-side outcomes improve. To validate overall performance under tight-deadline stress, we conduct experiments on a real federated LoRA trainer. FedRAS delivers 22\% more timely updates with 23\% fewer bytes than fixed rank 16 under deadline stress at statistically equivalent accuracy, while full-parameter updates miss every stress deadline; link-oblivious low-rank schedules trade adaptation capacity for delivery, and FedRAS holds the high-capacity end of that trade-off; and, at 500 clients, exact scheduling beats greedy heuristics whenever the budget binds, at sub-second per-round cost.
